  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "__cil_insert_avrule",
    "file": "selinux/libsepol/cil/src/cil_binary.c",
    "description": "`__cil_insert_avrule` accepts 32-bit `src`, `tgt`, and `obj` values but stores them without range validation into the 16-bit fields of `avtab_key_t`. In this PoV, a source type/type-attribute receives policydb value 65536; assigning it to `avtab_key.source_type` truncates it to zero. Later, neverallow checking assumes avtab type IDs are valid one-based indices and evaluates `p->attr_type_map[k->source_type - 1]` in `check_assertion_avtab_match`. With the truncated zero ID this becomes `attr_type_map[-1]`, and `ebitmap_match_any` reads the invalid bitmap's `node` field, producing the heap-buffer-overflow. The bitmap allocation itself is correctly sized for `p_types.nprim`; the malformed zero avtab key is the root cause.",
    "conditions": "Attacker-controlled CIL must cause more than 65,535 primary type/type-attribute symbols to be generated (the PoV uses nested/repeated block inheritance to amplify a small input), then emit an allow rule whose source has value 65536, plus a neverallow with overlapping class permissions so `check_assertion` scans that avtab entry. The 32-bit source value flows from the policydb type datum through `__cil_avrule_expand_helper` into `__cil_insert_avrule`, is narrowed to zero in the 16-bit avtab key, and the neverallow path indexes `attr_type_map` with `zero - 1`."
  },
